From e6bc2e2c3dc1e6157da242166e3d3a8eff29ca5c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alphi <fork@localhost>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 16:20:54 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] fix: gemjucewindow never waits on the message thread
 under the Pd lock

create() and destroyMess() run inside a Pd message, so their thread (audio
thread, or any thread that delivers a message under sys_lock) holds the Pd
lock. Both called MessageManager::callSync. If the message thread meanwhile
wants the Pd lock (the window's own key/modifier timer, a mouse event over
the Gem window, any plugdata GUI update), neither side moves: hard freeze.
Reproduced with a create/destroy loop; stacks: audio thread in
gemjucewindow::callOnMessageThread <- destroyMess, main thread in
pthread_mutex_lock <- gemjucewindow::Window::timerCallback.

destroy: the window is cut loose from its owner under the Pd lock (atomic
m_owner cleared) and deleted later on the message thread (callAsync).

create: always deferred. The Window is built on the message thread (directly
when create arrives there, else by a posted callAsync), under the Pd lock,
and parked; a Pd clock finishes the GL half (makeCurrent, createGemWindow,
title, size) on the Pd thread, where Gem renders. Messages before the window
exists are safe: dimen/offset reach a parked window or are read by the queued
construction; render returns early; destroy (or freeing the object) drops a
queued construction (atomic generation/alive ticket, checked before any Pd
call) and deletes a parked one; a second create while pending is refused.

Window callbacks (mouse, keys, timer, resized) go through withOwner(): return
at once when the window is cut loose (its Pd instance may be gone), else
select the instance, take the Pd lock, re-check the owner. resized() always
locks (the lock is recursive), no m_callingSync. The drag-resize flag lives
in the Window, so a destroy mid-drag cannot leave a later window stuck.

The context is released after every render() and after the GL setup, so a
frame or a destroy on another thread never meets a context current elsewhere.
The Pd instance is stored when the object is created and used for the
window, instead of libpd_this_instance() on whatever thread sends create.
